# Layer boundaries

Packages depend **only on strictly lower layers**. Same-layer and upward
dependencies are both banned. Enforced by `make layers` (ADR-0002).

| Layer     | Contains                              | May import                |
| --------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| 0         | Pure utils, types, config schemas     | nothing internal          |
| 1         | Adapters: db, storage, email, cache   | 0                         |
| 2         | Domain + application services         | 0–1                       |
| 3         | Transport: tRPC routers, REST handlers | 0–2                       |
| 4         | Apps                                  | 0–3, `ui`                 |
| `ui`      | Design system                         | 0 only                    |
| `tooling` | Build/lint config                     | never imported at runtime |

Declared per package: `{ "repo": { "layer": 2, "runtime": "node" } }`.
A `runtime: "browser"` package must not depend on a `node` one.

## Logic belongs in layer 2, not in a transport

```ts
// ❌ BAD — query and business rule inside a resolver
export const invoiceRouter = router({
  send: procedure.input(schema).mutation(async ({ input, ctx }) => {
    const invoice = await db.select().from(invoices).where(eq(invoices.id, input.id));
    if (invoice.total > 100_00) await notifyFinance(invoice);
    return invoice;
  }),
});

// ✅ GOOD — transport translates, service decides
export const invoiceRouter = router({
  send: procedure.input(schema).mutation(({ input, ctx }) =>
    sendInvoice({ actor: ctx.actor, invoiceId: input.id }),
  ),
});
```

Two same-layer packages that seem to need each other: move the shared piece down,
orchestrate from above, or inject a function — **except inside layer 0**, where
foundation packages may form a small DAG (`errors → types`, `contracts → utils`).
From layer 1 up, never add the dependency.
